paleology.en:
n. The study or knowledge of antiquities, esp. of
   prehistoric antiquities; a discourse or treatise on antiquities;
   archaeology .

palmary.en:
a. Palmar.
a. Worthy of the palm; palmy; preeminent; superior;
   principal; chief; as, palmary work.

perdue.en:
a. Lost to view; in concealment or ambush; close.
a. Accustomed to, or employed in, desperate enterprises;
   hence, reckless; hopeless.

perversion.en:
n. a curve that reverses the direction of something
n. an aberrant sexual practice; 
n. the action of perverting something (turning it to a wrong use)

perversions.en:
n. a curve that reverses the direction of something
n. an aberrant sexual practice; 
n. the action of perverting something (turning it to a wrong use)

phonolite.en:
n. A compact, feldspathic, igneous rock containing
   nephelite, hauynite, etc. Thin slabs give a ringing sound when struck;
   -- called also clinkstone.

phthalin.en:
n. A colorless crystalline substance obtained by reduction
   from phthalein, into which it is easily converted by oxidation; hence,
   any one of the series of which phthalin proper is the type.

pinic.en:
a. Of or pertaining to the pine; obtained from the pine;
   formerly, designating an acid which is the chief constituent of common
   resin, -- now called abietic, or sylvic, acid.

pirn.en:
n. A quill or reed on which thread or yarn is wound; a bobbin;
   also, the wound yarn on a weaver's shuttle; also, the reel of a fishing
   rod.

poly-.en:
a. A combining form or prefix from Gr. poly`s, many; as,
   polygon, a figure of many angles; polyatomic, having many atoms;
   polychord, polyconic.

polyclinic.en:
n. A clinic in which diseases of many sorts are treated;
   especially, an institution in which clinical instruction is given in
   all kinds of disease.

polytocous.en:
a. Bearing fruit repeatedly, as most perennial plants;
   polycarpic.
a. Producing many or young.

ponderings.en:
plural of pondering
v reflect deeply on a subject
s deeply or seriously thoughtful;

poppied.en:
a. Mingled or interspersed with poppies.
a. Affected with poppy juice; hence, figuratively, drugged;
   drowsy; listless; inactive.

precisive.en:
a. Cutting off; (Logic) exactly limiting by cutting off all
   that is not absolutely relative to the purpose; as, precisive censure;
   precisive abstraction.

prelibation.en:
n. A tasting beforehand, or by anticipation; a foretaste;
   as, a prelibation of heavenly bliss.
n. A pouring out, or libation, before tasting.

privateness.en:
n. Seclusion from company or society; retirement;
   privacy; secrecy.
n. The state of one not invested with public office.

prodigally.en:
adv. In a prodigal manner; with profusion of expense;
   extravagantly; wasteful; profusely; lavishly; as, an estate prodigally
   dissipated.

prodigiousness.en:
n. The quality or state of being prodigious; the state
   of having qualities that excite wonder or astonishment; enormousness;
   vastness.

pseudoscopic.en:
a. Of, pertaining to, or formed by, a pseudoscope;
   having its parts appearing with the relief reversed; as, a pseudoscopic
   image.

pustular.en:
a. Of or pertaining to pustules; as, pustular prominences;
   pustular eruptions.
a. Covered with pustulelike prominences; pustulate.
